l  how does this actually cool you off as  the sweat evaporates heat is pulled away  from your body and eventually your  temperature goes down so the more you  sweat the more sweat the will evaporate  and pull heat away from your body and  the more you sweat this one evaporates  and it pulls heat away from your body  and eventually you're back to normal  again  negative feedback reverses the change  that was happening to you  well what about positive feedback  positive feedback is a little more rare  positive feedback will actually  encourage or promote a change that's  happening to your body well before we go  through a biological example let's look  at again an example of positive feedback  in your home well throughout your home  you have smoke alarms scattered in most  rooms of your home let's say in the  middle of the night a fire breaks out in  the corner of the room and you're  sleeping so you don't even know it's  happening  well one of the smoke alarms will begin  to let off that loud piercing noise now  look at the definition of positive  feedback whatever change is happening is  going to be promoted and encouraged  so as this smoke alarm goes off it will  send a signal to all the other smoke  alarms and the alert is promoted the  alert is encouraged and so this you this  way you can see in this example you know  you can be woken up and you can get to  safety but positive feedback well  encourage change and promote it now  let's go through some biological  examples so a great example of positive  feedback are the muscle contractions  that begin in a woman's uterus when  she's about to give birth to a newborn  child these muscle contractions begin  very slow at first and then what happens  a signal gets sent to the woman's brain  the brain will respond by releasing a  hormone now ask yourself if this was  negative feedback what would happen to  the contractions look at the definition  negative feedback would reverse this  change do we want to reverse do we want  to reverse and stop these contractions  if we were to do that the baby would  never be delivered so in this case  positive feedback will encourage the  change positive feedback will promote  the the muscle contractions and so the  hormones released actually cause the  muscle contractions that speed up so the  baby will be delivered in birth in a  more timely manner  okay moving on we come to another  characteristic that all life shares and  that's the ability to reproduce and  develop well reproduction reproduction  is simply passing on DNA genetic  material from one generation to the next  we can break reproduction down to its  its most simplest form and that's  passing DNA to the next generation  well when we look at how life reproduces  well those that reproduce sexually you  know inside of the sperm cell there is  DNA from the the male and inside of this  egg cell there is DNA from the female  and so sexual reproduction has figured  out a way to combine DNA from the male  with the female but not every type of  organism reproduces sexually some  organism let's talk about the two types  of reproduction some organisms reproduce  asexually without another individual  here we have an animation of a cell  slowly dividing itself into two cells  and so when organisms reproduce  asexually they are genetically identical  to one another the offspring that are  created are genetically identical to the  parent and so we basically have cloning  by Nature here examples of organisms  that reproduce this way bacteria  Protista and even some fungus and some  plants and some animals however most  fungus plants and animals reproduce  reproduce sexually but let's look at a  quick example in animals here's an  animal by the name of a Hydra and it's  experiencing budding in the picture you  can see a new individual is growing off  of the body of the parent and eventually  it will pop off and float away in the  ocean and a sexually reproducing and  then there's sexual reproducing 